  "response": "## The finding\n\n`crime_00039` — **Oversized Raster**, `src/assets/hero-banner.png`, severity `low`, confidence `0.95`.\n\nEvidence, verbatim:\n\n> - `byte size: 260,000 bytes (253.9 KB)`\n> - `severity ladder: low ≥ 200 KB · medium ≥ 500 KB · high ≥ 1000 KB`\n> - `format: png`\n> - `consider resizing to the rendered dimensions and re-encoding to webp or avif`\n\n## Verdict: request changes, don't block\n\n253.9 KB is the *bottom rung* of the ladder — 47 KB over the 200 KB floor, and less than half the medium threshold. Nothing here is a correctness or safety issue; it's a page-weight issue with a quick, low-risk fix (`effort: quick`, `risk: low` on the suggested action). Blocking a landing-page PR over a single low-severity asset finding is disproportionate, and it trains people to route around the scanner.\n\nBut it shouldn't be waved through either, for one reason the finding's own score doesn't capture: **a hero banner is almost certainly the LCP element.** The detector scores by bytes alone — it has no idea where in the render path this file sits. 253.9 KB in a lazy-loaded footer illustration is genuinely `low`; the same 253.9 KB as the first paint on the landing page is the single biggest lever on that page's LCP. Treat this as low-severity-but-high-leverage: request the re-encode in this PR, since the fix is minutes of work and the asset is new (fix it before it has consumers).\n\n## One caveat on the evidence\n\nThe finding gives **bytes only — no pixel dimensions**. Compare `crime_00038` on `check-icon.png`, which does report `dimensions: 32 × 32 px`. So I can't tell you from the scan whether this is a 4000px-wide export being downscaled 3× in the browser, or an already-modest image that's simply encoded as PNG when it shouldn't be. That changes which half of the fix matters more, so check the intrinsic dimensions before picking numbers:\n\n```bash\nsips -g pixelWidth -g pixelHeight src/assets/hero-banner.png\n```\n\n## Target dimensions\n\nSize to the **largest CSS width the image actually renders at, times 2** for DPR — and cap there. Two rules:\n\n- **Cap the top at 2× the max render width.** For a full-bleed hero constrained to a ~1280px content column, that's **2560px wide**; for a hero that stays inside a 1200px container, **2400px**. Going past 2× is invisible on every shipping display and is the most common cause of exactly this finding. If the PNG comes back wider than that, the resize alone is most of the win.\n- **Ship a `srcset` rather than one file.** Emit **640 / 1024 / 1536 / 2048 (/ 2560)** widths and let the browser pick. A phone downloading a 2560px hero is the real waste; a single resized file doesn't fix that, and this is a *new* asset so there's no migration cost to doing it right now.\n\nBudget: **under 150 KB for the largest variant, ideally ~80–100 KB.** At 1600×800 (1.28 MP), a photographic hero lands around 60–90 KB in AVIF and 120–160 KB in WebP — both comfortably inside budget, which tells you the current 253.9 KB is roughly 3× what it needs to be.\n\n## WebP vs AVIF: use AVIF, with a WebP fallback in `<picture>`\n\n**AVIF** for the primary encode. For a hero — large, photographic, gradient-heavy, few hard edges — AVIF is where its advantage is largest: typically **30–50% smaller than WebP at visually equivalent quality**, because its chroma handling and intra-prediction are built for exactly this content. On a file that is likely the LCP resource, that delta is the whole point. Browser support is a non-issue now (Chrome, Firefox, Safari 16+, Edge 121+), and the `<picture>` fallback covers the tail regardless.\n\n```html\n<picture>\n  <source type=\"image/avif\" srcset=\"hero-banner-1024.avif 1024w, hero-banner-2048.avif 2048w\" sizes=\"100vw\">\n  <source type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"hero-banner-1024.webp 1024w, hero-banner-2048.webp 2048w\" sizes=\"100vw\">\n  <img src=\"hero-banner-1024.webp\" width=\"2048\" height=\"1024\" alt=\"…\"\n       fetchpriority=\"high\" decoding=\"async\">\n</picture>\n```\n\n**Two cases where I'd flip to WebP as primary instead:**\n\n1. **The hero contains crisp text, UI chrome, or a product screenshot.** AVIF's default chroma subsampling smears small colored text and thin lines in a way WebP doesn't. If someone can read words in this banner, encode WebP (or AVIF with `-yuv 444` and a higher quality target) and verify by eye.\n2. **The build can't do AVIF.** Encoding is meaningfully slower than WebP and needs a modern `sharp`/`libavif`. If this repo has no image pipeline — and the `raster_should_be_vector` and `svg_with_embedded_raster` findings on the two sibling assets suggest assets here are hand-committed — WebP-only is a perfectly good ship-today answer. Don't hold the PR for a build-system change.\n\nConcrete command, assuming a 2400px cap:\n\n```bash\nnpx sharp-cli -i src/assets/hero-banner.png -o hero-banner-2048.avif resize 2048 -- avif --quality 50\nnpx sharp-cli -i src/assets/hero-banner.png -o hero-banner-2048.webp resize 2048 -- webp --quality 80\n```\n\nStart at AVIF q50 / WebP q80 and only raise if you can see banding in the gradients — for a hero, q50 AVIF is usually indistinguishable from the source at arm's length.\n\n## Two things to add to the same review comment\n\nSince this is the LCP image and the PR is touching it anyway:\n\n- **Never `loading=\"lazy\"` on the hero.** Add `fetchpriority=\"high\"` instead — it's a bigger LCP win than the last 20 KB of compression.\n- **Set explicit `width`/`height`** so the browser reserves the box and you don't trade an LCP win for a CLS regression.\n\nOne related note for the author, outside this finding's scope: `crime_00029` flags `src/assets/partner-logo.svg` as **high** severity — an SVG with two base64 rasters embedded in it. That's a genuinely worse asset problem than the hero, and if the same design-tool export path produced both, worth fixing together.",
